			<div class="content" id="post-content-4818683">
									

<p>Let's see what we have here.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Here,&nbsp;have&nbsp;some&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;OK,&nbsp;now&nbsp;give&nbsp;me&nbsp;back&nbsp;my&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>First of all, you should specify that the &quot;true&quot; anomaly is a monster that can hide itself inside the mattress, as you told me in #thecritters. Just so reviewers don't <em>become confusion</em>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After several tests [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why didn't they check inside the mattress during this time? I think it would be some of the very first things they do. You may give the monster some &quot;hidding abilities&quot; to cover this up.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[&#8230;] primary researcher goes on sabbatical and the tests are paused [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is weird for me. I don't think the Foundation would stop the tests if this is a newly discovered anomaly. If the researcher goes on vacations, they would assign someone else. Maybe the staff member complaint about the floor in between tests?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I plan to have the diary entries of the child's mom (explaining the monster her child describes leading up to the murder) [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you should focus on this. The diary of a mother becoming more concerned about her child seeing monsters can be pretty terrifying, not to mention that you already &quot;know&quot; what's it like &quot;to have a monster inside your mattress&quot;. But the thing that bugges me is that you'll have to describe the monster and its abilities in the Description, so it will not be as surprising as it needs to. Maybe you can write the &quot;old&quot; document and then add an addendum saying the new discoveries and that the document is pending updates?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>a D-class who was moved to this room after repeatedly complaining about every roommate they were assigned.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Nope. The Foundation is a prison, not an student dormitory. If the D-Class don't like their &quot;roommate&quot;, that's up to them. The Foundation would only separate them if they're a risk to each other. Maybe you can still write the experiment log without this part?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, this has some plot holes and inconsistencies, but I think it can work as a Horror article.</p>
<p>Feel free to contact a member of the <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/butterfly-squad-roster">Butterfly Squad</a> to get more crit, or PM me to take another look.</p>
<hr />
<p>I got summed back.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Here,&nbsp;have&nbsp;some&nbsp;more&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;OK,&nbsp;now&nbsp;give&nbsp;me&nbsp;back&nbsp;my&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So my concern is that this might fall under the category of &quot;just another spooky monster&quot;. I was wondering that <em>maybe</em> it would be better to never show the monster. At all. Maybe the Foundation <em>knows</em> that there is a monster, but they never get to see it? Maybe they just discover the bodies and eventually the figure out that there <em>is</em> a creature hidding in the mattres?</p>
<p>Also, just finding the bodies of the researchers would feel a little sour. Maybe you can write the transcription of video logs from the surveillance cameras? That way you could add some more spooky atmosphere.</p>
<p>Furthermore, I think you may want to read <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/essays-by-a-hack-terror">this essay about horror in the SCP format</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, this can be very spooky, but be careful to not fall under &quot;just another monster&quot;.</p>
<p>Feel free to contact a member of the <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/butterfly-squad-roster">Butterfly Squad</a> to get more crit, or PM me to take another look</p>
<p>(I highly recommed reaching out to other people, since I can't hand out greenlights yet.)</p>

							</div>

								
