			<div class="content" id="post-content-4823659">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Behold!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>A dark take on getting a bad back from an old mattress. After a grisly murder where a child's spine was torn out of her body (and is missing), crime scene investigators noted the lack of blood on her twin size mattress, although the sheets were saturated. The SCP Foundation secures the mattress as SCP-XXXX. But what if the mattress itself isn't the problem?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not a bad start. I'm just confused as to why the Foundation would deem the mattress as an SCP or why they're even involved at all. I feel like you should start this off as some sort of a law enforcement investigation. The oddities found during that prompt the Foundation to look into this and eventually get involved.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Journal entries describe the following story: a mom gets a mattress from a freecycling site when her older daughter is ready for a &quot;big girl bed.&quot; The child first complains that her mattress is bumpy, then that it moves at night, and finally runs into her room after dreaming about a skeletal creature in her mattress that &quot;wants my back, because its back hurts.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While I do really like this, I feel like journal entries wouldn't convey this successfully. I feel that this should be don't by a comprehensive description of the mattress during an investigation and interviews with the mother.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When the mattress is cut open, there is no sign of what is causing the lumps.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A bit cliche, but nothing world-ending.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When it was discovered that the SCP could move, researchers investigated locations near the mattress for potential causes of the anomaly. They discovered that an elderly man with lordosis (swayback) passed away near the murdered child. He was known to say he'd sell his soul to have a young person's spine again. Relationship not yet established</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure how I feel about this section. There are pros and cons to having it. The monster being able to switch mattresses is scary, but you already have this story about a child dying and this other section could potentially overshadow that. Focus on one element, I would recommend the story of the child and mother and do it well instead of spreading yourself thin.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They discovered that an elderly man with lordosis (swayback) passed away near the murdered child. He was known to say he'd sell his soul to have a young person's spine again. Relationship not yet established</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels a bit cartoonish juxtaposed against what you previously have. Also, not everything <em>needs</em> to be explained. Horror comes from not showing the monster. That being said, you can have the monster send some form of a parting message using the bumps. I imagine them realizing that the bumps spell out the word &quot;MORE&quot; or something like that.</p>
<p>Overall, this has legs. I'd like for you to clean up the idea and streamline your narrative. This will help with really concentrating those horror elements while not taking away from the narrative you're proposing. I'll give a tentative <span style="color: green"><strong>greenlight</strong></span> as long as you take into account some of my recommendations. Good luck!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

